.sp
.in 8n
Shows the regular file $PREFIX/lib/usilic/HELP.en.epub (\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/HELP.en.epub etc.) as an \fBEPUB\fR. Except with the -g (GUI) option or a start from the icon, the font type, size, line spacing, letter spacing and so on cannot be changed. On Windows, which starts only in GUI, this is no problem; elsewhere, restart as needed with ^Og (the CUI / GUI toggle key) or the -g option.
.br
.sp
^G (or a right click of the mouse) in the text of \fBHELP\fR moves to the list of chapters. This is the contents file following \*[usilic-i]nav.xhtml\*[usilic-ie] in the .epub format (required by the EPUB3 specification); for text without contents such as HELP.en.epub, usilic(1) treats lines ending with an added tab code as chapter titles, so typing ^O^J^S^Q^I$^M (show the list of lines ending with a tab code searched with \fBEGREP\fR) gives the same list. By the way, ^Jp^Q^I$^M (split the \fBHELP\fR screen with \fBPART\fR) also shows a list that looks the same, but a manual is easier to follow when read straight through, so \fBHELP\fR does not use it. Moving to the previous / next chapter with the ; / : keys works the same, though, in any \fBEPUB\fR including HELP.en.epub.
.br
.sp
^Os on the \fBHELP\fR screen switches the scroll \fBmode\fR between continuous and per chapter (only one chapter on the screen).
.br
.sp
^[[ / ^[] change \fILINE-HEIGHT\fR, and ^[{ / ^[} change \fILETTER-SPACING\fR, to increase / decrease the line spacing and the letter spacing (the width of one half-width character) respectively (twice that for Japanese). The setting is kept for the next start.
.br
.sp
^Ot switches between serif and sans-serif. What becomes serif is, in \fBEPUB\fR, the characters the CSS specifies as serif, and on other screens, full-width characters (Japanese); half-width characters stay in the monospaced font. The setting is kept for the next start.
.br
.sp
^J^F shows the list of \fBFONT\fR (monospaced) and \fISANS-SERIF\fR / \fISERIF\fR (with Japanese) fonts found by fc-list (the current font is underlined). When started from a terminal, \fBFONT\fR begins with "terminal font"; choosing it empties \fIFONT\fR and goes back to the terminal's font. Choosing with ^M or a left click switches at once and writes it to the \fIFONT\fR setting.
.br
.sp
In HELP.en.epub, the blue numbers (such as [1]) after the names of the setting items in \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] are mainly links to notes; ^M (or a click) jumps to one, and ^G (or a right click of the mouse) returns to the original place.
.br
.sp
^T^H opens \fBHELP\fR in another window as a child-process usilic(1) (same as -H). Operations in the child usilic(1) are not kept in the history, so the child's history lists are almost empty.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBKEY\fR          ：key assignments\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
           ^K：show the list of keys
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^K：start \fBKEY\fR from a prompt
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^T^K：open \fBKEY\fR in a child window (same as -K)
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
It looks like \fBHELP\fR after ^O^S : ^M (\fBFGREP\fR with a full-width colon), but ^M (or a left click) on the highlighted area runs the function shown, and the h key jumps to the place in \fBHELP\fR that explains it.
.br
.sp
^T^K opens \fBKEY\fR in another window as a child-process usilic(1) (same as -K). A usilic(1) started this way as a pager (next chapter) shows "USILIC" in the bottom right area of the screen, and "(parent's PID)" is added at the end of the first line (the command line) in \fBINFO\fR (to tell it from the original usilic(1)).
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBEPUB\fR         ：view Epub files\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Os：switch the scroll \fBmode\fR
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^O^C：record a range (vertical text)
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Je：type a file name
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^J^E：choose from the history list
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^J^F：choose from the font list
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Ow：cycle how Web links are opened
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Typing a work number (N1234AB etc.) of "Shosetsuka ni Naro", its sister site "Shosetsu wo Yomo!" or the adult "Nocturne Novels" or its work URL (https://ncode.syosetu.com/n1234ab/ etc.), a Kakuyomu work ID (digits only) or work URL (https://kakuyomu.jp/works/ID), the URL of an Aozora Bunko library card (https://www.aozora.gr.jp/cards/person number/card work number.html), or the URL of an Archive of Our Own work (https://archiveofourown.org/works/work number) makes it into an \fBEPUB\fR with \fInarou2epub\fR and opens it (needs Epub in \fIURL\fR); typing an http(s) URL handles it in the current ^Ow \fBmode\fR, the same as a Web link in the text. The ^J^E list also shows no files other than .epub, except for URLs of usilic-web (see src/web/README): one typed at ^Je appears in the ^J^E list too, and http://localhost/usilic-web/ is there from the start. The entrance URLs of "Shosetsuka ni Naro", "Nocturne Novels", Kakuyomu, Aozora Bunko and Archive of Our Own are also there from the start, and choosing one opens it in the current ^Ow \fBmode\fR (in a browser etc. when the \fBmode\fR is Epub).
.br
.sp
When the current \fBmode\fR of ^Ow is Epub, it shows the list of the server's bookshelf, and the chosen book is downloaded to \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic-web\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]texts\*[usilic-ie]/ and then opened. On localhost a book already there is opened from there; in the other \fBmode\fRs, it opens in a browser etc.
.br
.sp
Web links (http etc.) are handled according to the \fIURL\fR setting; each ^Ow switches to the next of the \fBmode\fRs below and shows its name on the mode-line (any key clears it). The default is "Open", and it returns to the default after handling a link.
.br
.sp
^O^C in vertical text starts COPY (COPY is shown on the mode-line); the range dragged with the mouse is highlighted, and releasing the button asks Record?(y/n). y records the string and its place in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]epub-marks.txt\*[usilic-ie] (in the same "book.epub:path of chapter:place: string" format as the records of usilic-web), and any other key keeps the range without recording (^G ends COPY).
.in 0
