.sp
.in 8n
When (1) ends with "/" and (5) is a non-reversed "usilic-1.5b" (\fB(ls)\fR below), pressing ^G repeatedly goes back and forth between the previous directories. In an externally started usilic(1) (^Ou of \fBUSILIC\fR), (5) is a non-reversed "USILIC".
.br
.sp
In \fB(ls)\fR, a line starting with "d" ("drwxr-xr-x ..." etc.) and ending with "/" is a directory. ^M moves there, and selecting "./" or ^Xd"."^M (\fBDIR\fR) refreshes the listing of the current directory. The following keys can also be used to change directories.
.in 0
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^]：move to the current directory
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
   ^J^C：move to the current directory
.in 0
.in 19n
Moves to \fB(ls)\fR of the current directory even while viewing a file or while \fBSHELL\fR is running. Pressing ^] or ^J^C again in \fB(ls)\fR refreshes the listing, just like selecting "./".
.in 0
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
   ^J^B：move to the parent directory
.in 0
.in 19n
Like ^J^C, moves to the parent directory even while viewing a file or while \fBSHELL\fR is running.
.in 0
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
    ^JN：move to the Nth directory
.in 0
.in 19n
Counting the leading "/" (slash) of the displayed string as 0, moves to the directory at the Nth "/". For example,
.in 0
.sp
.in 23n
/\*[usilic-i]Users\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
^J2 there goes to the following,
.in 0
.sp
.in 23n
/\*[usilic-i]Users\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
and ^J^B instead of ^J2 to the parent directory
.in 0
.sp
.in 23n
/\*[usilic-i]Users\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
respectively. When the input is on the mode-line at the bottom of the screen and the string starts with a slash, it moves to the corresponding directory. For example, after viewing \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] with ^Xf"~/.usilic/usilicrc"^M (\fBFILE\fR) and typing ^X^F,
.in 0
.sp
.in 23n
/\*[usilic-i]Users\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie]
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
^J2 on this cursor line moves to
.in 0
.sp
.in 23n
/\*[usilic-i]Users\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
\&.
.in 0
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^M：show the file contents
.in 0
.in 19n
A line starting with "-" ("-rxwr-xr-x ..." etc.) is a file (regular file). ^M shows the contents of the file, and if the file's suffix is set in \fIFILTER\fR, shows the output of the given program (a .gz file through "gzip -dc", etc.).
.br
.sp
For a suffix set in \fILAUNCH\fR, usilic(1) leaves its screen once and runs the given program (an .info file with "info -f", etc.).
.br
.sp
When the file size exceeds the value set by \fIBUFFER\fR, the message "Not enough buffer" is shown and the file is opened the same way as ^O^M (described later), reading only what fits on the screen (any key clears the message).
.br
.sp
For a file judged binary by the \fICODE\fR setting, the first control code found and the file type from file(1) are shown. On macOS, however, images, PDFs and the kinds Quick Look can handle (.numbers .docx etc., see below) open in Quick Look instead of the file(1) display, and videos (.mp4 .mov .mkv etc.) open not in Quick Look but in IINA (VLC if missing, the default app if neither). Videos selected with .[] are passed together and played in turn (␣ as well), and a setting in \fILAUNCH\fR takes priority (on WSL2 the Windows default app handles them).
.br
.sp
In GUI, image files (.jpg .jpeg .png .gif .bmp, only the first frame of a GIF) are shown on the usilic(1) screen (shrunk to fit the width and height of the screen; small images are not enlarged). Pressing ^L while showing one reloads it, which helps to check an image you have exported again (reopening also reloads it).
.in 0
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
   ^O^M：read the file one line at a time
.in 0
.in 19n
A file selected with this key is read line by line, only as far as shown (for huge files or archive content lists). It is not kept in the access history. It works the same way in the \fBLOCATE\fR, \fBDU\fR, \fBFILE\fR and \fBUSILIC\fR lists.
.in 0
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
   ^[^M：show control codes as they are
.in 0
.in 19n
Control codes 0x7f and below 0x20 are shown reversed (0x0d as a reversed "M"). It is not kept in the access history, and the \fIFILTER\fR setting is not applied. Any key interrupts the reading. It works the same way in the \fBLOCATE\fR, \fBDU\fR, \fBFILE\fR and \fBUSILIC\fR lists.
.br
.sp
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
   ^J^J：back to the previous file
.in 0
.in 19n
Like ^G in \fB(ls)\fR, goes back and forth with the previous file.
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Switching the \fB(ls)\fR display
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
    ^Oo：switch the ls(1) format
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
    ^Ol：cycle the sort key
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
   ^O^L：reverse the sort
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
^Oo switches the ls(1) output format, but when the file names are shorter than 8 bytes, the display is fixed to file names only. Each ^Ol switches the order of the ls(1) output through Date ⇒ Size ⇒ Kind ⇒ Name, and ^O^L sorts in reverse.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Selecting files
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
  .：toggle selection of the current line
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
 ^␣：toggle selection of the current line (only this one in the \fBFILE\fR and \fBDIR\fR lists)
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
  [：select the current line, then up (\fBMACRO\fR)
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
  ]：select the current line, then down (\fBMACRO\fR)
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
^Oy：select all lines
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
^On：deselect all lines
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
  %：select lines whose name matches a regular expression (\fB(ls)\fR only)
.in 0
.in 8n
.in 13n
.ti 8n
  *：select lines with the same extension (\fB(ls)\fR only; the cursor line's extension by default)
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
In \fB(ls)\fR, a selected line gets an underline and becomes a target of file operations/viewing/extraction (without a selection, viewing/running applies to the cursor line). The same operations work in the \fBLOCATE\fR, \fBDU\fR, \fBFILE\fR, \fBDIR\fR and \fBUSILIC\fR lists. After \fBCP\fR/\fBMV\fR/\fBARC\fR, the processed files are underlined in \fB(ls)\fR of the copy/move/extract destination (a temporary selection). It is a target of ^Jt etc. just like a normal selection. The temporary selection disappears with ^L, and can also be removed with . or ^On.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBCP\fR/\fBMV\fR        ：copy/move files\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Oc：copy
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Om：move
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
^Oc/^Om shows the prompt "Destination? " on the mode-line. Type the copy/move destination and run with ^M (^M alone means \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]data\*[usilic-ie]/, other relative paths are based on the current directory, and ^W inserts a previous directory name). The work is done in \fBSHELL\fR; on success, after moving to \fB(ls)\fR of the destination, the processed files are underlined there too. Nothing is done when nothing is selected.
.br
.sp
On macOS extended attributes are copied too, so copying to a USB stick etc. may bring along files starting with "._" (\fICP\fR). On Windows it is done with copy, xcopy and move of cmd.exe (del and rmdir for \fBRM\fR), and the \fICP\fR, \fIMV\fR and \fIRM\fR settings are not used.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBRM\fR           ：delete files\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Or：delete
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Typing "y"^M at the ^Or prompt "Delete? (y/n)" deletes the selected files. Any other input does nothing. If a target violates permissions (read-only etc.), "Permission denied" is shown and nothing is done. The progress remains in \fBSHELL\fR and can be checked with ^[s or ^O^Js.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBARC\fR          ：view and extract archive contents\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Oa：extract an archive
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^A：choose from the history list
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Oe：EPUB content list
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^O^E：list of the records
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Selecting a file with one of the suffixes below with ^M in \fB(ls)\fR shows its content list (^O^M also shows the list, but items cannot be selected). Choose one with ^M to view its contents (an archive inside shows its own list).
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
\&.tar .tar.gz .tgz .taz .tar.z .tar.bz2 .tbz2 .tar.xz .txz .tar.zst .tzst .lzh .zip .jar .rpm .deb .rar .7z
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
As in \fB(ls)\fR, ␣ shows the file of the cursor line in Quick Look, and after selecting several with the .[] keys, ^Oa extracts them to the destination you give (the whole archive if nothing is selected). ^Oa on a file with the same suffix in \fB(ls)\fR also extracts the whole archive.
.br
.sp
As with \fBCP\fR/\fBMV\fR, ^M alone without a destination extracts to \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]data\*[usilic-ie]/, but \fBARC\fR cannot start when several files are selected in \fB(ls)\fR. A file whose content list cannot be read is opened as a normal file. On Windows, everything except zip is handled with the tar.exe that comes with Windows (.rpm .deb cannot be handled).
.br
.sp
^J^A lists only the archive files in the access history (\fBFILE\fR), and choosing one shows its content list (.epub goes to the ^J^E list).
.br
.sp
Choosing an .epub file with ^M in \fB(ls)\fR starts \fBEPUB\fR (choosing an .epub with ^O^M shows, as with \fBARC\fR, a list whose items cannot be selected). Even if the .epub suffix is set in \fIARCHIVE\fR, \fBARC\fR does not open it, but choosing the .epub with ^Oe lets you choose an item from the content list and view it as with \fBARC\fR (without layout). The .epub lists opened this way are shown with ^O^E, and in the \fBFILE\fR history list, (epub) or (unzip) respectively is added after the file name. The ^Je prompt accepts only .epub files in the current directory (no history list is shown, so try completing with ^I).
.br
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBTOOL\fR         ：run a program\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Jt：type a program name
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^J^T：choose from the history list
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
The ^Jt prompt is shown with the file name of the cursor line (all of the selection, if any) already in it, so type the program name in front and run it with ^M. A program listed in \fIPROGRAM\fR runs after leaving the usilic(1) screen once, and you come back to the original screen when it ends. A program not in the list runs in \fBSHELL\fR. If you start in \fBSHELL\fR an app that changes the terminal settings, such as an editor, restart \fBSHELL\fR with ^Js. A command starting with "usilic" does not run in \fBSHELL\fR even if it is not in \fIPROGRAM\fR; it starts another usilic(1) as an external pager (treated like ^Ou of \fBUSILIC\fR below, and kept in history with "(usilic)"). ^J^T shows a sorted list of the programs run so far from \fBTOOL\fR and \fBSHELL\fR, and ^R then shows the history in the order run.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBUSILIC\fR       ：view files\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Ou：view
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Tn：bring the next window to the front (the original and external usilic(1)s)
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Tp：bring the previous window to the front (likewise)
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
^Ou shows the underlined files (the cursor line if nothing is selected) in a window of another usilic(1) started as an external pager. In CUI on Linux too, when a display (X11 or Wayland) is available, it opens in a new window of the current terminal (GNOME Terminal, Konsole, xterm etc., or whichever is found if unknown). In other CUI cases (e.g. over SSH), quitting the external pager brings you back to the original screen.
.br
.sp
Several files and directories open one by one in separate windows (over SSH etc., the next one opens when the previous one quits). A directory opens in an \fB(ls)\fR just for that occasion (history is neither read nor written, and the original usilic(1) keeps running). ^Tn / ^Tp bring the original usilic(1) and the externally started windows to the front in turn (usable from any of those windows). 
.br
When the original usilic(1) exits, everything it started externally exits too (each can also be quit on its own with q, ^X^C etc.). The files and directories started externally stay in the original usilic(1)'s \fBUSILIC\fR, ^X^F and ^X^D histories with "(usilic)" appended, so you can reopen them later (they are not used as the place ^J^J or ^G returns to, nor by ^W in the \fBCP\fR/\fBMV\fR destination). In lists such as \fBLOCATE\fR, the selection (the highlighted line if none) is shown the same way.
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
.ti 0n
Modes            ： can start outside \fB(ls)\fR too\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Before starting a new \fBmode\fR, usilic(1) pushes the current mode-line on a stack and then updates the screen (^G returns to the last one pushed). The stack is cleared when you choose a directory in \fB(ls)\fR or change directories with ^J2 etc., and the label "usilic-1.5b" is shown at mode-line(5). Choosing a directory in a list such as \fBLOCATE\fR or \fBDU\fR gives the \fB(ls)\fR screen, and choosing a further directory there also clears the stack. Pressing the same key that started the \fBmode\fR inside that \fBmode\fR, such as ^J^L during \fBLOCATE\fR, discards the history in between (^G still returns to where the \fBmode\fR was first started) and starts the \fBmode\fR again.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBHISTORY\fR      ：access history\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
           ^R：show the history list
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Pressing ^R at the \fBFILE\fR, \fBDIR\fR, \fBMAN\fR, \fBLOCATE\fR, \fBFGREP\fR, \fBEGREP\fR, \fBPART\fR or \fBSHELL\fR prompt shows the \fBHISTORY\fR list of the strings saved in the order typed (newest on the bottom line). Pressing ^M on the cursor line, or just typing, puts that string into the prompt. Strings typed from \fBTOOL\fR and \fBSHELL\fR are saved in the \fBTOOL\fR list, and ^R shows the list of commands run. Pressing ^R again on the list switches between the sorted list without duplicates and the \fBHISTORY\fR list in the order typed. In \fBEGREP\fR and \fBPART\fR, the first ^R shows a list with the regular expression examples written in \fIGREP\fR at the top (the cursor on the regular expression used last). The history is saved in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]history\*[usilic-ie] and can be used after the next start. The number of lines kept for each kind is set by \fILINE\fR (unlimited by default).
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
F/\fBEGREP\fR      ：search for strings
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
             ：search for strings\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	       ^S：incremental search
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^J^S：search with an extended regular expression
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^O^S：list of matching lines (literal)
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	   ^O^J^S：list of matching lines (extended regular expression)
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
The incremental search of ^S searches with grep -F (\fBFGREP\fR). Each time a character is typed a literal search is done, and ^M confirms it. When nothing matches, "No String" is shown.
.br
.sp
^J^S confirms the extended regular expression typed at the prompt with ^M and then searches with grep -E. Matched strings are highlighted, and ^N/^P move to the next/previous match.
.br
.sp
^O^S / ^O^J^S list the matching lines of the current screen, and ^M jumps to the chosen line (Emacs occur). At the place jumped to, the matches are highlighted as with ^S, and ; / : move to the previous/next item.
.br
.sp
From the body of a vertical \fBEPUB\fR, the list is vertical too.
.br
^G goes back to the list, then to the original screen. For extended regular expressions, examples of the main metacharacters (special characters) that mean the same in both BSD grep on macOS and GNU grep on Linux are registered in the list shown by ^J^S. They are as follows.
.br
.sp
.in 8n
Positions and boundaries
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
 ^ : start of a line (e.g. ^A matches an A at the start of a line)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
 $ : end of a line (e.g. Z$ matches a Z at the end of a line)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
\eb : word boundary (e.g. \ebcat\eb matches "cat" alone, not in "concatenate")
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
\eB : anything but a word boundary
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Any character, sets of characters (character classes)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
    . : any one character except a newline
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
  [ ] : any one of the characters in the brackets (e.g. [abc] is one of a, b, c)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
 [^ ] : one character not in the brackets (e.g. [^0-9] is one non-digit)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
[a-z] : a range (e.g. [a-z] is a lowercase letter, [0-9] a digit)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
   \ed : any digit (same as [0-9])
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
   \eD : any character but a digit (same as [^0-9])
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
   \ew : letters, digits and underscore (about the same as [a-zA-Z0-9_])
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
   \eW : anything but letters, digits and underscore
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
   \es : any white space (space, tab, newline etc.)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
   \eS : any character but white space
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Number of repetitions (quantifiers)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
    * : 0 or more times (may be absent, or any number)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
    + : 1 or more times (at least once)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
    ? : 0 or 1 time (may or may not be there)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
  {n} : exactly n times (e.g. \ed{4} is 4 digits)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
 {n,} : n or more times
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
{n,m} : at least n and at most m times
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Grouping and alternation (OR)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
    | : OR (e.g. cat|dog is "cat" or "dog")
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 20n
.ti 12n
  ( ) : grouping; makes a string one unit that can be reused (captured) later
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Escaping (\e)
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
\e. : search for a dot itself
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
\e? : search for a question mark itself
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 17n
.ti 12n
\e( : search for a parenthesis itself
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
grep -E has no lazy matching (*? +?), non-capturing groups (?:), or lookahead/lookbehind ((?=)(?!)(?<=)(?<!)).
.br
.sp
.in 8n
Reference
.in 0
.in 12n
In basic regular expressions (plain grep), some metacharacters must be written with a \e (backslash). usilic(1) searches do not use them, but they are listed here for when you meet them in other tools.
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 28n
.ti 16n
       ¥+ : 1 or more times
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 28n
.ti 16n
       ¥? : present or not
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 28n
.ti 16n
¥( ... ¥) : group several characters into one
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 28n
.ti 16n
  ¥{n,m¥} : n to m times
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBPART\fR         ：split a buffer\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Jp：split the buffer at the given string
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^P：choose from the history of splits
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Type a search string, and the buffer being shown is searched with an extended regular expression (grep -E, as in \fBEGREP\fR); the buffer is split at the lines containing a match, and a contents list of those lines is made. The mode-line of the contents list shows "(egrep)", and while showing a part chosen with ^M, the mode-line shows "\fBPART\fR: search string".
.br
.sp
When the file shown in \fBFILE\fR is split, ^G returns to that file, and the \fBFILE\fR access history gets both the file name and 'file name "string"'. Choosing the latter repeats the same split and shows the top line of the contents list (or the place last viewed, if any), and ^G goes back to the contents list, the file, then \fB(ls)\fR.
.br
.sp
^J^P shows only the 'file name "string"' entries of the \fBFILE\fR history. ^S / ^J^S also search the headings on the contents list screen.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBDU\fR           ：disk usage\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Jd：total the disk usage with du(1)
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^D：back to the result screen
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
When \fBDU\fR is started with ^Jd, du(1) is run on the current directory and a list sorted by size is shown. Pressing ^G while totalling interrupts it and moves to the result so far.
.br
.sp
^J^D only goes to the existing result, so the list is not updated even if the current directory has changed.
.br
.sp
Pressing ^Jd in the \fBDU\fR list totals again for the cursor line, and for a file, for the current directory.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBLOCATE\fR       ：find file locations\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Jl：build the database for locate(1)
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^L：back to the \fBLOCATE\fR screen
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
At the mode-line prompt, ".tgz"^M, for example, shows the names of all files in the system with the .tgz suffix. The prompt holds the previous search string, so ^M as it is shows the previous result without searching again.
.br
.sp
^Jl builds (updates) the database. Pressing ^G while building interrupts it, and you can search with the database built so far.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBMAN\fR          ：view manual pages\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Jm：show a manual page
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^M：show the history list
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
When the typed string matches an existing page, the manual is shown with man(1).
.br
.sp
^J^M shows the manual page chosen from the history list, or the one for a newly typed string.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBFILE\fR         ：type a file name\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Xf：type a file name
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^X^F：choose from the history list
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Shows the file typed on the mode-line, or chosen from the list of files accessed so far. In the \fBFILE\fR list, a file name gets "(program name)" when a program was used, and a string used by \fBPART\fR is shown in double quotes ("). Files selected with ^O^M or ^[^M in \fB(ls)\fR, however, are not shown by the ^J^J key or in the \fBFILE\fR list.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBDIR\fR          ：type a directory name\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Xd：type a directory name
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^X^D：choose from the history list
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Moves to the directory typed on the mode-line, or chosen from the list of directories accessed so far. ^G returns to the list, but typing a directory-changing key such as ^J^C, ^J^B or ^J2 leaves \fBDIR\fR. In both \fBFILE\fR and \fBDIR\fR, a path that does not exist shows "No such file or directory" and returns to the previous screen. 
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBUSILIC\fR       ：access history lists\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^[u：contents
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^J^U：list by kind
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Ju：list in time order
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^X^W：save the buffer to a work file (WORK)
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Lists the history of files, directories and manual pages accessed so far. The ^[u contents list the headings \fBMAN\fR, \fBDIR\fR, \fBFILE\fR, \fBTOOL\fR, WORK and \fBUSILIC\fR, and ^M opens each list.
.br
.sp
^M on a \fBTOOL\fR item puts that string into the \fBTOOL\fR prompt. WORK is the list of work files saved with ^X^W. \fBUSILIC\fR is the list of files and directories started externally with ^Ou (or usilic via ^Jt), with "(usilic)" after each name (they appear the same way in the \fBFILE\fR and \fBDIR\fR lists and the list in time order).
.br
.sp
^M on an item with "(usilic)" (also in the ^X^F and ^X^D lists) reopens it externally: a file as an external pager, a directory as an external \fB(ls)\fR (you can also select several and open them together with ^Ou).
.br
.sp
^J^U is a sorted list of all kinds, and ^Ju is a list in the order accessed (newest on the bottom line). In the lists, the same file operations as in \fB(ls)\fR (.[], ^Ou, ^Oc etc.) can be used.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBMACRO\fR        ：keyboard macro settings\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^X(：start recording a keyboard macro
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^X)：stop recording the keyboard macro
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Xe：run the recorded macro
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^Xs：save the recorded macro
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
The keys pressed from ^X( to ^X) are recorded (the \fBmode\fR name on the mode-line is \fBMACRO\fR meanwhile), and ^Xe repeats them. The interval between keys can be set with \fIWAIT\fR.
.br
.sp
^Xs right after ^Xe saves the recorded keys to the "--" line of \fIMACRO\fR, shows the \fIMACRO\fR settings list, and asks for the key (one character) to run it. Pressing a key sets it as "M-N key K-n ..." and puts "--" on the next line (it takes effect from the next start). If not right after ^Xe, it is the editing \fBmode\fR for the existing settings. ; : [ ] e l are already set as follows.
.in 0
.sp
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	        ;：^G^P^M  open the previous item
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	        :：^G^N^M  open the next item
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	        [：.^P     select and go up
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	        ]：.^N     select and go down
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	        e：^[e     \fBEDIT\fR
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	        l：^[l     log of \fBSHELL\fR
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
COPY         ：copy strings\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^O^C：start the \fBmode\fR
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	    ^@/^␣：toggle start/end of a range
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^X␣：toggle start/end of a rectangular range
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^[w：copy the ranges to the clipboard
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^[t：copy the ranges and put them into the \fBTOOL\fR prompt
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^O^Y：insert the clipboard contents while typing at a prompt
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Copies strings on the screen other than the mode-line to the clipboard. After ^O^C, move the cursor with ^N/^P/^F/^B/^A/^E (or the arrow keys); after starting a range with ^@, the area highlighted as the cursor moves becomes the area to copy. After ending the range with ^@, another ^@ creates more areas, and the same toggling works with ^X␣ (rectangular range). ^[N (N is a number) moves to line N, and ^G cancels COPY. ^[t also copies all areas to the clipboard, but then ends COPY, opens the \fBTOOL\fR prompt (^Jt), pastes the copied text, and waits for a program name. File names, URLs etc. on the screen can be edited into a form to pass to a program and run as they are with ^M. Several non-rectangular areas are joined with a space instead of a newline (the highlighted J). All the areas made are copied to the clipboard with ^[w and can be pasted with ^O^Y. On macOS they are also copied to the system clipboard, so they can be pasted into other apps, but when "Universal Clipboard" is on, they also reach the other devices of the same Apple Account. To avoid unexpected strings being pasted, turn "Handoff" off in "System Settings" ⇒ "General" ⇒ "AirDrop   Handoff".
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBEDIT\fR         ：edit a file\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	      ^[e：edit with the environment variable $EDITOR
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
When the file name (mode-line(1)) is a file and the program name (mode-line(5)) is blank, starts the editor defined in \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie]. The current line number N is passed to the editor with the +N option, so an editor that accepts this option is convenient. pico(1) is set by default.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBSHELL\fR        ：virtual terminal\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^[s：go to \fBSHELL\fR
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Js：restart \fBSHELL\fR
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^J.：go to the current directory
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^J,：list of the current directory
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^Jw：show the buffer name
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^W：show the buffer contents
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
          ^O/：search the output
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^O^F：page down
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^O^B：page up
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^O^L：redraw
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
^J. sets the current directory of the \fBSHELL\fR prompt to the place of \fB(ls)\fR (^J, runs "ls -l"). ^J^W saves the buffer contents in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]data\*[usilic-ie]/ (or WORK) and inserts "cat file name" into the prompt. Inside \fBSHELL\fR, ^O/ searches the output, ^O^F/^O^B page down/up, ^O^L redraws, and ^J^I gives \fBINFO\fR. ^G returns to the original \fBmode\fR. When the prompt has not come back because of an abnormal state or a running program, trying a function that uses the \fBSHELL\fR prompt, such as \fBCP\fR or \fBTOOL\fR, or \fBEXIT\fR switches to the \fBSHELL\fR screen. When usilic(1) is started at the \fBSHELL\fR prompt with a pipe ("ls | usilic"^M) or a file argument ("usilic file"^M), the contents are shown in the current usilic(1) with the file name "(stdin)" or "file" respectively, and with a directory argument ("usilic directory"^M) it becomes \fB(ls)\fR of that directory. The output of "man bash"^M etc. is also shown as "(stdin)", since a temporary PAGER environment variable turns it into piped input to usilic(1). This feature uses the shell's process ID to authenticate the socket(2) used for switching \fBmode\fRs, so if su(1) etc. is run at the \fBSHELL\fR prompt, the \fBmode\fR does not switch from that prompt (see socket(2) in the NOTES chapter). The \fBSHELL\fR of usilic(1) is a virtual terminal provided for file operations such as \fBCP\fR/\fBMV\fR/\fBRM\fR, so it has only minimal features, and programs that control terminal settings, such as editors and pagers, do not work properly. A program whose name is in the \fIPROGRAM\fR list, however, runs after switching the screen to \fBTOOL\fR. If you start another such program by mistake, quit it with a key if possible, or otherwise restart \fBSHELL\fR with ^Js. The supported shells are bash, zsh, tcsh and csh. The shell that started usilic (the environment variable \fBSHELL\fR) is used, and bash when started from any other shell (on Windows, Git Bash's bash when started from Git Bash, otherwise cmd.exe).
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Completion
.in 0
.in 12n
^I right after the \fBSHELL\fR prompt shows the list of executable files. This is the list of executable files under the directories in the environment variable $PATH. When you choose one, or a program whose name matches the typed string exists on $PATH, it is inserted at the cursor; if there are several candidates, a beep tells you. Another ^I then shows those candidates, and the same steps narrow down the program. After the program name is settled, ^I after ␣ shows the contents of the current directory, so the same steps work for the arguments; completion of executable names with ^I also works at the ^Jt (\fBTOOL\fR) and ^Jm (\fBMAN\fR) prompts. \fBFILE\fR and \fBDIR\fR do not search executables on $PATH. Only files and directories in the current directory are completed.
.in 0
.sp
Command line     ： mainly at prompts\*[usilic-tab]
.br
.sp
.in 8n
The keys below can be used only at a prompt starting with "usilic-1.5b:" (a temporary PS1 environment variable).
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^W：show the ^J^W, ^Jw command again
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^C：cancel the input
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
The keys below can also be used at the text input prompts of \fBSHELL\fR and the mode-line.
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^H：delete one character left of the cursor
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^K：delete from the cursor to the end of the shown line
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^U：delete from the cursor to the start of the shown line
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^Y：insert the deleted text at the cursor
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^D：delete the character at the cursor
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^E：to the end of the shown line
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^A：to the start of the shown line
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^B：one character left
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^F：one character right
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^I：complete the file name
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^L：redraw the screen
.in 0
.in 10n
.in 19n
.ti 10n
     ^Q：type a control character
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBINFO\fR         ：usilic(1) information\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
           ^I：show the main information
.in 0
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
         ^J^I：run from \fBSHELL\fR
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Shows the following information about usilic(1).
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
.in 27n
.ti 12n
OS name : the command line it was started with (e.g. "macOS Sequoia 15.7.8 : usilic -c"; followed by "(parent's PID)" in an externally started usilic(1))
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 27n
.ti 12n
Startup file : settings file
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 27n
.ti 12n
       \fBERROR\fR : error messages while running
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 27n
.ti 12n
       \fBSHELL\fR : output from the virtual terminal
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 27n
.ti 12n
        DATA : \fB(ls)\fR of \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]data\*[usilic-ie]/
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 27n
.ti 12n
        WORK : work files
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Each of them can also be opened directly with the keys below.
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
.in 19n
.ti 12n
^J^Ou：\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie]
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 19n
.ti 12n
^J^Oe：\fBERROR\fR
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 19n
.ti 12n
^J^Os：output of \fBSHELL\fR
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 19n
.ti 12n
^J^Od：\fB(ls)\fR of \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]data\*[usilic-ie]/
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 19n
.ti 12n
^J^Ow：list of WORK (same as WORK in the \fBUSILIC\fR contents)
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
\fBEXIT\fR         ：quit usilic\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^X^C：quit
.in 0
.in 19n
.ti 0n
\*[usilic-tab]	     ^[^[：quit 
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Shows "Exit? (y/n)" on the mode-line, and "y"^M quits usilic(1). When usilic(1) is started with a directory argument, each \fBmode\fR reads information such as the cursor position at the last quit from \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]history\*[usilic-ie] and restores the same state. When a \fBmode\fR ends, the state is saved to the same file for the next run, and usilic(1) itself, when started with neither standard input nor arguments, restores the state at the last quit. All of this history goes through the single file \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]history\*[usilic-ie], so to start several usilic(1) with the same arguments, kill(1) the running usilic(1) and start again. If you are switched to the \fBSHELL\fR screen in the "ＳＨＥＬＬ busy" state, end the program and then run \fBEXIT\fR again with the ^X^C or ^[^[ key.
.in 0
.sp
SETTINGS\*[usilic-tab]
.br
.in 4n
\*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie]
.in 0
.in 8n
usilic(1) reads \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] at startup to set up its environment. If the file does not exist, \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] is copied from \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/ to \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/ and then read. For the setting items and their contents, see the comments in that file.
.in 0
.sp
BOOT OPTIONS\*[usilic-tab]
.br
.in 4n
usilic [file] [< file ...] [directory]
.in 0
.in 13n
[-c|-g] [-s|-S usec ...] [-d|-D dot ...]
.br
[-f|-F font] [-z pt] [-w|-W wait] [-b|-B size]
.br
[-l|-L line] 
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 9n
.ti 4n
-g : start in a GUI window
.in 0
.in 9n
On macOS, when \*[usilic-i]usilic.app\*[usilic-ie] is opened from the Finder (no terminal), it starts in GUI even without this option. -c and -g cannot be given together.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 9n
.ti 4n
-H : start with \fBHELP\fR open in a child usilic(1)
.in 0
.in 9n
Starts as if ^Jt"usilic \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/HELP_en.1"^M had been run (\*[usilic-i]HELP.1\*[usilic-ie] when LANG is ja_JP.UTF-8, and \fBHELP\fR itself then starts in Japanese). The parent starts as usual in the \fB(ls)\fR of the current directory or the screen at the last quit, and the child shows \fBHELP\fR as a plain-text pager (\fBmode\fR line (groff)), so you can refer to the child while working in the parent. The child window opens to the right of the parent, aligned with its top (to the left of the parent at the right edge of the screen). The parent comes to the front last. In GUI the children open with the same size, font and colors as the parent; in Terminal.app they open with the same profile as the parent. A child can also end by itself (q, ^X^C, ^[^[, or closing the GUI window). While running, ^T^H opens the same child.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 9n
.ti 4n
-K : start with \fBKEY\fR open in a child usilic(1)
.in 0
.in 9n
Like \fBHELP\fR with -H, opens \fBKEY\fR (a list of only the lines containing "：", highlighted up to the "："; ^M runs the key in the parent's screen, and h moves the \fBHELP\fR child to that line) in a child window. While running, ^T^K opens the same child.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
The options below change a \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] setting for this run only. The uppercase ones do the same and also write the value to the setting in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie], so it applies afterwards without the option.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 23n
.ti 4n
-s usec ...（-S）: speed of inertial scrolling (\fISPEED\fR)
.br
Gives the 10 speeds of inertial scrolling, from the slowest, as the wait for each line (microseconds). Only as many levels as given are replaced, and smaller values are faster (default : 90000 20000 10000 5000 3000 2000 1000 500 200 100).
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 22n
.ti 4n
-d dot ...（-D）: drag distance that starts inertial scrolling (\fIDRAG\fR)
.br
.in 22n
Gives, from level 1, the distance (dots) that decides at which level of speed inertial scrolling starts when a mouse drag is released. For example, with -d 20 40 80 160 320, a movement just before release of less than 20 dots stops, 20-39 gives level 1, and so on (default : 1 2 4 8 16 30 40 50 60 70).
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
-f font（-F）: font file used in GUI (\fIFONT\fR)
.in 0
.in 19n
.in 24n
.ti 19n
Example: -f /Library/Fonts/MS-Gothic.ttf
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
-z pt: size of the text in GUI (points, 4-200; the last size if omitted, 18 at first)
.br
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
-w wait（-W）: interval of running macros (\fIWAIT\fR, in units of 10 msec)
.br
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
-b size（-B）: buffer size (\fIBUFFER\fR, in MB, 1-100)
.br
.sp
.in 4n
.in 19n
.ti 4n
-l line（-L）: number of history lines (\fILINE\fR, 0 is unlimited)
.br
.sp
.in 4n
.in 9n
.ti 4n
-c : start in a terminal (CUI)
.in 0
.in 9n
The default when started from a terminal. Inertial scrolling with the mouse is implemented too. The level of coasting when a drag is released is decided by the number of lines moved, and the \fIDRAG\fR setting is not used. For the speed, see \fIARROW\fR.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 9n
.ti 4n
-e file : make an \fBEPUB\fR from \fBHELP\fR.1 and exit
.in 0
.in 9n
Writes \fBHELP\fR.1 as an \fBEPUB\fR to the standard output and exits. For example, usilic -e \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\fBHELP\fR_en.1 > ~/\fBHELP\fR.en.epub makes an \fBEPUB\fR with the same contents as the \fBHELP\fR of ^H. The cover image and the note texts (the \*[usilic-i]usilicrc\*[usilic-ie] comments, default values, etc.) are inside \fBHELP\fR.1 too, so \fBHELP\fR.1 alone is enough.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
.in 9n
.ti 4n
-h : show the usage and exit
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
GUI (-g)
.in 0
.in 8n
Starting
.in 0
.in 12n
Started with -g, usilic(1) runs in a window separate from the terminal. It quits when the window is closed or ^C is pressed in the terminal that started it (also when started as a pager).
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Display
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 24n
.ti 12n
cmd + "=" : larger text
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 24n
.ti 12n
cmd + "-" : smaller text
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 24n
.ti 12n
cmd + "0" : reset the text size
.in 0
.in 12n
.in 24n
.ti 12n
cmd + "k" : switch the color scheme (gray on black, green on black, amber on black, black on white)
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
The text size can also be changed with ctrl + "=" / "-" / "0" (when the Windows key cannot be used instead of cmd, such as in WSL on Windows). Resizing the window changes the numbers of lines and columns to match. The line height (the space between characters in vertical \fBEPUB\fR) can be changed with \fILINE-HEIGHT\fR.
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Mouse
.in 0
.in 12n
Moving the mouse moves the cursor line with it, and a click on the highlighted place selects the item. Grabbing the screen and dragging scrolls it, and inertial scrolling continues at the speed of the mouse pointer when released. After the scrolling is stopped with a click or the enter key, the item is opened even if the mouse pointer is not on the highlighted line. The mouse wheel scrolls the screen in the direction it is turned. ← / → start inertial scrolling (→ upward). During inertial scrolling upward, ↑ speeds up and ↓ slows down. Going further in the reverse direction from the slowest speed gives the slowest speed in the reverse direction. In vertical \fBEPUB\fR the arrow keys swap up/down and left/right, and ↓ starts scrolling to the left (←/→ adjust the speed). ^M during inertial scrolling only stops it and does not open the highlighted line. The speed can be adjusted with -s and the starting distance with -d.
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Dropping files
.in 0
.in 12n
Dropping a file from the Finder onto the window opens that file (\fB(ls)\fR of it for a directory). When several files are dropped, they are opened through a list of the files, with the file on the top line open (^G to the list). The same happens when \*[usilic-i]usilic.app\*[usilic-ie] is chosen with "Open With" in the Finder.
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
Mouse in a terminal
.in 0
.in 12n
In CUI too, in terminals that pass mouse operations to apps (Terminal.app, iTerm2 etc.), scrolling with drags and the wheel can be used, and the former uses the \fIARROW\fR settings. Starting inertial scrolling with ←/→ (↓/↑ in vertical \fBEPUB\fR) is also the same as in GUI.
.in 0
.sp
NOTES\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
Environment\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 8n
When usilic starts for the first time, it creates the work directories \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/ and /\*[usilic-i]tmp\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/ (defined in \*[usilic-i]config.sh\*[usilic-ie] of the source). usilic(1) uses the "USILIC" environment variable to tell whether the current terminal is \fBSHELL\fR. In particular, usilic(1) cannot start as a file manager in a terminal where this variable is defined. To use it as a pager, "export PAGER=usilic"^M in the terminal lets you read the output of dvi2tty(1) or man(1) in usilic(1). In \fBSHELL\fR, however, the value of this "PAGER" environment variable cannot be changed.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Kanji code\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 8n
File contents are shown in UTF-8 (the environment variable USILICCHARSET can change it to euc, sjis, jis or ascii). Lines that are valid UTF-8 are shown as they are, and lines containing the sjis (shift-jis), euc or 7bit-jis (ISO-2022-JP) kanji code are converted to UTF-8 (sjis and jis via euc). The code is judged line by line: jis is decided as soon as its escape sequence is found, and for sjis and euc the line is checked to its end and the code found more often is chosen. So lines that use many one-byte half-width kana etc. may not be judged correctly.
.br
.sp
When USILICCHARSET is ascii, nothing is converted. ^[M (raw display) also does this conversion for what is shown on the screen, but ^X^W on a file opened with ^[M saves the original contents before conversion, without wrapping at the screen width and in the kanji code as it is, so use it when you want to keep e.g. a diff file containing sjis code in its original kanji code (^X^W on a file opened with ^M etc. saves the converted UTF-8 text on the screen).
.br
 
.br
.in 4n
Interprocess communication\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 8n
socket(2)
.in 0
.in 12n
When the output of a program is redirected to usilic(1) at the shell prompt of the \fBSHELL\fR chapter, that usilic(1) (a child process of the shell) creates a socket(2) whose file name contains the shell's process ID, and writes the redirected contents there. The usilic(1) that started the shell (the parent process of the shell), on finding that socket(2), reads the redirected contents from it with the file name "(stdin)". When usilic(1) is started with a file argument from the shell prompt, a file with the same name is used instead of a socket(2).
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
pipe(8)
.in 0
.in 12n
Apart from the socket(2) above, interprocess communication through pipe(8) is used with the child process fork(2)ed to read the contents of a file into the buffer.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Typing a backslash (\e)\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 8n
The \e (backslash) used in regular expressions of \fBEGREP\fR, \fBPART\fR etc. can be typed on a macOS JIS keyboard (Japanese layout) by pressing the ¥ key while holding option. To always type \e, change "Characters to type with the ¥ key" of "Japanese - Romaji" in "System Settings" ⇒ "Keyboard" ⇒ "Input Sources" to "\e (backslash)"; the ¥ key alone then gives \e (¥ with option). On a US keyboard, type it as is with the \e key above (or left of) enter.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Web pages to PDF
.in 0
.in 8n
The two scripts below in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/ (installed by make install) make a Web page into a single tall PDF page as high as the whole page (the text can be selected). They run a headless Chromium with Playwright, so the preparation in \fIURL\fR above is needed, and urls2pdf also needs pypdf.
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]venv\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]bin\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]pip\*[usilic-ie] install pypdf
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
url2pdf URL [directory]
.in 0
.in 12n
Makes the page at URL into a PDF, saves it as "page title.pdf" in directory (\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]Downloads\*[usilic-ie] if omitted), and prints its path (with -2 etc. added if the name exists). Pdf for Web links in \fBEPUB\fR (see \fBEPUB\fR above) uses this.
.in 0
.sp
.in 8n
urls2pdf [option] URL
.in 0
.in 12n
Makes each link target in the table-of-contents page URL into a PDF, and combines them into one PDF with the contents page itself first (with a bookmark for each chapter). Links are collected from the page after Chromium shows it, so links made by JavaScript are also taken. By default, links to the same site in the main text (inside <main>, <article> etc., or else including the headings, up to just before the number of links jumps by merging with a sidebar etc.) are taken in the order they appear. Links forbidden by robots.txt are not taken, and pages that need a login cannot be taken. The PDF of each link is saved in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]Downloads\*[usilic-ie]/contents title/, the combined PDF in \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]Downloads\*[usilic-ie]/contents title.pdf, and the path of the combined PDF is printed at the end (progress on standard error).
.in 0
.sp
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
         -l : only show the list of links to take (URL and text)
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
   -m regex : take only links whose URL matches this (e.g. -m 'help/')
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
         -a : take links from the whole page, not only the main text
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
         -x : also take links to other sites
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
     -w sec : seconds to wait for each link (default: 1)
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
     -d dir : where to put the PDF of each link
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
    -o file : path of the combined PDF
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
 --no-index : do not include the contents page itself
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
 --no-merge : do not combine into one (print the path of each PDF)
.in 0
.in 16n
.in 30n
.ti 16n
         -h : show the usage
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
For example,
.in 0
.sp
.in 16n
\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]urls2pdf\*[usilic-ie] -l https://example.com/guide/
.in 0
.sp
.in 12n
checks the links to take; narrow them with -m if there are too many, add -a or -x if too few, then run without -l. It can also be used from ^Jt or \fBSHELL\fR. A PDF page has a size limit (about 200 inches), and a longer page is split into several pages.
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Making \fBEPUB\fRs from "Shosetsuka ni Naro", Kakuyomu, Aozora Bunko and Archive of Our Own
.in 0
.in 8n
\fInarou2epub\fR N-code|Kakuyomu work ID|work URL|Aozora Bunko library card URL|Archive of Our Own work URL ... [-o directory]
.in 0
.in 12n
Makes a "Shosetsuka ni Naro" work (also novel18.syosetu.com works such as Nocturne Novels, passing the age check), a Kakuyomu work or an Aozora Bunko work (the URL of the library card or the XHTML version; made from the XHTML version with ruby, with gaiji turned into Unicode characters where possible) into a vertical EPUB3, saves it in directory (\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]data\*[usilic-ie]/ if omitted, opened with ^J^Od) as "title_N-code(work ID)_date.epub" and prints its path (progress goes to standard error; it waits 2-7 seconds between pages, so it takes time in proportion to the number of episodes). Works for both serials and short stories, with the cover, title page, synopsis, each episode's foreword/text/afterword and illustrations (for Kakuyomu, the catchphrase and introduction as the synopsis, and only the text of each episode). For a work on Archive of Our Own (an English-language fan fiction site), it does not extract the text but downloads the horizontal \fBEPUB\fR the site itself provides as it is and saves it as "ao3-work number-title.epub" (adult works are fetched after passing the confirmation; works shown only to logged-in users cannot be downloaded).
.br
.sp
Run inside \fBSHELL\fR (e.g. ^Jt"\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]narou2epub\*[usilic-ie] n1234ab"^M), it opens the finished \fBEPUB\fR in the \fBEPUB\fR screen as it is. Epub for Web links in \fBEPUB\fR (see \fBEPUB\fR above) also uses it. It is a modified NarouToEpub3 (Apache License 2.0, \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]narou2epub-assets\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]LICENSE\*[usilic-ie]) and needs the following preparation. Use the \fBEPUB\fRs you make only for your own enjoyment and do not distribute them (the copyright of each work belongs to its author).
.in 0
.sp
.in 16n
python3 -m venv \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]venv\*[usilic-ie]
.br
\*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].local\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]lib\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]venv\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]bin\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]pip\*[usilic-ie] install lxml ebooklib requests Pillow
.in 0
.sp
.in 4n
Required software\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 8n
usilic(1) uses external programs such as groff(1) (formatting \fBHELP\fR and \fBMAN\fR), mg(1), info(1), 7zz(1) (.7z in \fBARC\fR) and w3m(1) (Dump/Term for Web links in \fBEPUB\fR). On macOS, IPA Gothic (font-ipafont), the default font of GUI, is also needed (when installed, it becomes the default font when \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]Applications\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]usilic.app\*[usilic-ie] is clicked to start). At the first start, if any of them are missing, it switches to \fBSHELL\fR and runs the install command ("HOMEBREW_NO_ASK=1 brew install ..." on macOS, "sudo apt install -y ..." on Ubuntu) automatically (in GUI, the cover of \fBHELP\fR and an "installing" notice are shown above, and \fBHELP\fR opens when it finishes. Each one is offered only once).
.br
.sp
On macOS without Homebrew (brew), the install command of Homebrew itself (the one at https://brew.sh) is put instead. Homebrew is installed after asking for your password, and when the prompt comes back, "brew install ..." is run next. To install by hand, run the command at https://brew.sh in a terminal and then add brew to PATH as the guidance shown says (on Apple Silicon, append "eval "$(/\*[usilic-i]opt\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]homebrew\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]bin\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i]brew\*[usilic-ie] shellenv)"" to \*[usilic-i]~\*[usilic-ie]/\*[usilic-i].zprofile\*[usilic-ie]).
.br
.sp
Homebrew and building usilic itself (make) need the Xcode Command Line Tools (compiler, git etc.). The Homebrew installer installs them if missing, but to install them first, run "xcode-select --install" in a terminal (Xcode itself from the App Store is not needed). Whether the Command Line Tools are installed can be checked by whether "xcode-select -p"^M prints a path. If brew or make fails with "invalid active developer path" etc. after updating macOS, reinstall them with "xcode-select --install"^M.
.br
.sp
On Windows it runs in WSL2 (Ubuntu inside Windows). The build steps are the same as Linux, and the GUI window appears on the Windows desktop through WSLg (Japanese text needs fonts-noto-cjk, which is added to the guidance at the first start if missing). On WSL2, videos and Web links in \fBEPUB\fR (Open) open in the Windows default app, and the contents of COPY go to the Windows clipboard.
.br
.sp
In Git Bash, usilic(1) built with MSYS2 runs (GUI and \fBLOCATE\fR cannot be used). Opening outside and the clipboard connect to the Windows side as on WSL2. See docs/BUILD.md in the source for the build steps.
.in 0
.sp
AUTHOR\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
usilic@X.com
.in 0
.sp
SEE ALSO\*[usilic-tab]
.in 0
.in 4n
groff(1), man(1)
.in 0
.br
